00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:10.570
video platform that Wendover is a part of.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:14.900
I need not tell you what happened in March,
2020.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.060
Whether you’re watching this now or far
in the future, that date will forever remain

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.390
in people’s minds as when the century’s
first pandemic emerged.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.560
It will remain in people’s mind’s as when
the era of social distancing and isolation

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:28.699
began.

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:32.710
It will remain in people’s minds as when
they got sick, or when a loved one got sick,

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:33.710
or worse.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:38.840
In addition, though, for the millions of people
worldwide working in the aviation industry,

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:44.640
March 2020 will mark the date when they lost
their jobs, when their company started turning

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.690
south.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:48.580
It will mark the beginning of the end.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:54.160
There is perhaps no industry more severely
impacted by COVID-19 than aviation.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.670
As it is a brand new virus, in these early
days, when there is no immunity or vaccines,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:05.260
the only way to stop or slow its spread is
through physically isolating people.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:10.580
That is intrinsically in opposition to what
aviation is—aviation works to bring people

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.610
together.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:15.610
That meant that one of the first measures
governments implemented to slow the spread

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.890
was to ban travel.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:22.190
When the virus first emerged, its transmission
primary occurred in China, with only isolated

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:25.320
cases elsewhere from those who had travelled
from the epicenter.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:29.430
For this issue, the solution was simple—ban
travel from China.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:34.299
A number of countries, notably including the
US, barred foreigners who had recently been

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:38.900
in China from entering their borders and,
in response to the sharp decline in demand,

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.630
a number of airlines cancelled their flights
to greater China.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:47.140
The financial implications of this were limited
as few foreign airlines are significantly

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.780
exposed to China—partly due to the strength
of the Chinese airlines.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.490
Most of those mainland Chinese airlines, meanwhile,
are government-owned so they could take the

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:57.490
hit.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:01.550
Of course nearby Asian airlines like Scoot,
Cathay Pacific, and ANA would struggle, but

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:05.729
the lockdown of China was more or less a weatherable
hit.

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:11.009
But then the virus spread: first to South
Korea, so airlines cancelled flights to South

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:16.260
Korea, then to Iran, so airlines cancelled
flights to Iran, then to Italy, so airlines

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.760
cancelled flights to Italy, but then it seeped
through Italy porous borders to take hold

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.120
of the entirety of Europe.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.840
That was the moment when, to airlines, the
situation became severe.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:33.040
Europe is home to, by revenue, three of the
world’s six largest airlines—the Lufthansa

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.150
Group, Air France/KLM, and IAG.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:41.420
To have a virus devastating their home territory
was an untenable situation—nobody wants

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:43.650
to travel to the center of a Pandemic.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.940
But soon, the untenable situation became worse.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:52.380
On Wednesday March 11th, televisions switched
on to the American president sitting behind

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.300
his desk in the Oval Office.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:59.700
A speech in this setting is reserved for only
the most solemn occasions.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.879
Only four such addresses occurred in the entire
2010s decade.

00:03:03.879 --> 00:03:08.849
The president laid out some steps in his administration’s
response to the virus, the most stunning of

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:14.290
which being a complete ban on all foreign
travelers from Europe.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:17.069
That was unprecedented.

00:03:17.069 --> 00:03:21.900
Europe and the US are two of the most culturally
and economically linked regions in the world

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:27.209
and never before would anyone even imagine
anything even close to a complete travel ban

00:03:27.209 --> 00:03:28.879
between the two.

00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:31.849
The amount of individuals this would impact
was astronomical.

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:37.090
The North Atlantic is home to one of the busiest
flows of long-haul flights in the world.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:42.730
More than 1,700 aircraft per day cross the
Atlantic—many of those enormous wide-body

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:43.750
jets.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:47.010
These are some of the most profitable flights
for airlines out there.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.420
To imagine that this would all just come to
a stop was unthinkable.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:56.040
Now, Delta, American, and United would join
the big European three to have their demand

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.489
slashed by the virus.

00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:03.659
All six of the world’s largest airlines,
by revenue, went into crisis mode.

00:04:03.659 --> 00:04:08.519
Beyond just the transatlantic flying, intra-Europe
and US domestic traffic figures dropped off

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:09.620
a cliff.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:14.170
As one example, United Airlines, the fourth
largest airline in the world, issued a press

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:19.330
release announcing a 60% reduction in their
flight capacity in April, 2020.

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:24.120
Even with those drastic cuts, they predicted
an average load-factor—how full their planes

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.430
are—of 20-30%.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:30.610
Since then, they’ve cut flights even further.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:35.330
Most every airline in Europe started experiencing
net-negative bookings.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:39.270
That means that they were refunding more bookings
than they had coming in.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:45.220
Some airlines, including Qantas, Virgin Australia,
WestJet, and more decided to cancel all but

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.080
their domestic flights.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:52.660
Other airlines, including LOT, Brussels Airlines,
and Austrian Airlines decided to just completely

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:56.080
close down for a few weeks or months.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.930
They would fly no flights at all.

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:04.550
Since its occurrence, 9/11 has been the defining
crisis for the aviation industry.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:10.000
Since civil aviation began, traffic numbers
just grew and grew and grew until suddenly,

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.210
on a sunny September morning, that all stopped.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:18.520
It was bad, and airlines did go bankrupt,
but overall, the industry eventually made

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:20.500
it out the other end.

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:25.200
Less than a week after Coronavirus became
a Pandemic, though, one thing became abundantly

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:31.540
clear to airlines—Coronavirus, to them,
financially, was worse than 9/11.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:38.060
This was the most severe drop-off in passenger
demand in the history of aviation.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:41.580
Airlines are no strangers to doing things
that lose money.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.630
There is perhaps no industry out there that
so robustly embraces the idea of a loss-leader

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:48.310
than aviation.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:53.120
Airlines will go to great lengths to retain
loyalty—both of individual travelers and

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:54.980
of their corporate contracts.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:59.321
For example, airlines can and often do keep
routes flying in the depths of winter, when

00:05:59.321 --> 00:06:03.500
traffic is the lowest, in order to reliably
present themselves as the go-to airline for

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:04.500
their customers.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.850
If, say, there’s a US-based company with
significant amounts of travel to Barcelona,

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:13.040
they’re only going to set up a corporate
contract with an airline that can fly their

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:14.070
employees there year-round.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:19.789
Therefore, American Airlines, for example,
might fly there for twelve months a year even

00:06:19.789 --> 00:06:22.050
if only eight of those turn a profit.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:26.800
That’s because, with the loss of corporate
contracts, they might only turn a profit flying

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:32.120
to Barcelona five months a year, so overall
yearly profit will be higher by taking a loss

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:33.789
four months a year.

00:06:33.789 --> 00:06:38.580
The equation changes when nobody, including
those corporate customers, wants to travel,

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:39.580
though.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:43.240
Therefore, with the Coronavirus reaching pandemic
level in one of the lowest-traffic periods

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.949
of the year, airlines quickly were able to
make the decision to cut loss-leading long-haul

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:49.400
flights.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:50.990
These cuts are deep.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:55.820
The Lufthansa group, for example, the seventh
largest airline in the world by passengers,

00:06:55.820 --> 00:07:00.320
is reducing its long-haul schedule to a sole
flight from Zurich to Newark, then flights

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:06.130
from Frankfurt to Sao Paulo, Newark, Chicago,
Montreal, Tokyo, Bangkok, and Johannesburg.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:11.530
To think that this is what the seventh largest
airline in the world in 2020 looks like is

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:17.220
unimaginable, but the situation at the world’s
largest airline, American Airlines, is even

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.000
more severe.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.320
This is their long-haul network during the
Coronavirus.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:28.699
They’ll fly just Miami to London, Dallas
to London, and Dallas to Tokyo.

00:07:28.699 --> 00:07:32.560
This is a situation without a lot of good
news.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:38.050
Jobs will be lost and airlines will go bankrupt,
but there is plenty of hope that the industry

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:39.860
can survive.

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:45.259
While this fall will be far, airlines do have
further to fall than after 9/11.

00:07:45.259 --> 00:07:51.099
In 1999, United, American, and Delta achieved
operating margins—the ratio of their earnings

00:07:51.099 --> 00:07:56.370
to revenues—of 6.8, 6.5, and 12.7 percent,
respectively.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:08:02.620
In 2019, those margins were 9.9, 6.6, and
14.0 percent.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:07.120
These are small differences, but a few percentage
points can mean the difference between life

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.050
and death for an airline.

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:13.139
Another saving grace for airlines is the recent
plummet in oil prices.

00:08:13.139 --> 00:08:17.330
This is the byproduct of a price war going
on between two of the world’s largest oil

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:20.200
producers—Saudi Arabia and Russia.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:24.550
The two countries had long worked together
to limit output based on demand and therefore

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:29.590
more or less control oil prices, but, after
Russia refused to reduce their output, Saudi

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:34.269
Arabia responded by slashing prices and increasing
production in order to price Russia out of

00:08:34.269 --> 00:08:35.269
the market.

00:08:35.269 --> 00:08:40.569
Therefore, the same barrel of jet fuel that
would have cost airlines $60 in February costs

00:08:40.569 --> 00:08:43.180
only $40 in March, 2020.

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:47.980
That’s a significant discount on airlines’
biggest cost and, if it were not for the biggest

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:52.579
drop in demand in the history of aviation,
they would be celebrating a huge increase

00:08:52.579 --> 00:08:53.779
in profits.

00:08:53.779 --> 00:08:58.579
In the midst of the crisis, though, they’re
taking the small win of smaller losses.

00:08:58.579 --> 00:09:03.690
The aviation industry, as a whole, never truly
recovered from the effects of 9/11.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:07.879
That’s because “recovery” implies getting
back to normal, and that’s something that

00:09:07.879 --> 00:09:13.129
never happened after 9/11, and certainly will
never happen after Coronavirus.

00:09:13.129 --> 00:09:18.220
It’s nearly impossible to predict what will
happen in the aftermath of a situation never

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:19.759
seen before.

00:09:19.759 --> 00:09:23.389
As we’ve seen already and will certainly
continue to see, the response to extraordinary

00:09:23.389 --> 00:09:26.449
circumstances is extraordinary measures.

00:09:26.449 --> 00:09:32.209
Therefore, the industry will emerge from the
other end something completely new and novel.

00:09:32.209 --> 00:09:37.660
How deep the dip sinks will be determined
by how high the curve soars and that will

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:43.100
be determined by world governments, organizations,
and more than all, how every individual out

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:47.220
there responds to COVID-19.

